The voice came up through the brick the way water comes up through
a floor.
    I had my palm flat on the north wall of the Armory, and the wall
had stopped being a wall. It had turned into a pane of glass onto some‐
where else: a riverbank at night, cold, reeds nodding in the light of a
house I couldn’t see. A woman in a work jacket stood at the water’s
edge with something wrapped in linen clutched against her chest. Be‐
hind her a tall man in a wool cap held a flashlight and a flask with the
grim patience of someone for whom tea was the only thing in the even‐
ing he could control.
    “Niamh,” I said, out loud, to a wall.
    Her mouth moved. I caught my name, and a question after it, and
lost the question.
    “Don’t pull,” said Yanaisa. Her hand was on my forearm. “She isn’t
a rope. Let her stay where she is.”
    I’d been pulling. I’d mistaken an answer for something to reel in.
    I let the river be a river.



Mama Tete tapped her cane twice on the carpet.
  “The middle,” she said. “Where the forty sat.”



